---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../data/site';
import FormatFlow from '../components/charts/FormatFlow.astro';
import DecompositionFigure from '../components/charts/DecompositionFigure.astro';
import GapDiagram from '../components/charts/GapDiagram.astro';

const endpoints = [
  ['GET', '/health', 'liveness, the analyzer version, and the sample limit'],
  ['GET', '/', 'the same page rendered by the service itself, with a form'],
  ['POST', '/analyze', 'a trace in the body, a report out'],
];

const limits = [
  ['Request body', '8 MiB'],
  ['Samples per request', '2,000,000'],
  ['Wall-clock budget per request', '20 s'],
  ['Concurrent requests', '1 — the free instance has a tenth of a core'],
  ['Retention', 'none: the trace is held in memory for the request and then dropped'],
];
---

<Base
  title="The hosted analyzer — PACE"
  description="Send a trace to the PACE service over HTTP and get the attribution report back. The same open-source analyzer, running on a machine you did not have to build a Rust toolchain for."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">The hosted service · free, and the code is the same code</div>
      <h1>Post a trace, get the attribution back</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The analyzer is a Rust binary that reads a file. To try it you would have to install a
        toolchain, clone the repository and build it. This is that same binary behind an HTTP
        endpoint, so you can find out what PACE says about a run before you decide to embed anything.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        It is not a different product and it is not a hosted-only feature: it is
        {' '}<span class="mono">pace analyze</span> with the report returned over the wire, running
        from the same source you can read and deploy yourself.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#try">
          <i class="ph ph-arrow-down"></i>
          How to call it
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={site.repo}>
          <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
          Read the source it runs
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Live status</div>
      <h2>Is it awake?</h2>
      <p>
        This is a free instance on a shared host, which means two things you should know before you
        rely on it: it sleeps after fifteen minutes without a request, and the first request after
        that takes about a minute to answer. An uptime monitor pings it every five minutes to keep
        that from happening, and the state below is read from the service itself rather than from
        this page's build.
      </p>
      <div class="readout">
        <div class="readout-head">
          <span class="mono">{site.service}/health</span>
          <span>fetched by your browser, not by the site build</span>
        </div>
        <div class="readout-body">
          <div class="status-list" id="service-status">
            <dt>state</dt>
            <dd id="st-state">checking…</dd>
            <dt>analyzer</dt>
            <dd id="st-version">—</dd>
            <dt>uptime</dt>
            <dd id="st-uptime">—</dd>
            <dt>sample limit</dt>
            <dd id="st-limit">—</dd>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        If it says unreachable, the instance is probably spinning up — wait a minute and reload. If it
        stays unreachable, <a href={site.issues}>tell me</a> rather than assuming PACE does not work;
        the failure here would be mine, not the method's.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="try">
      <div class="section-label">How to call it</div>
      <h2>One POST, one report</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-terminal-window"></i> Terminal report</h3>
          <pre><code>{`curl -sS ${site.service}/analyze \\
  --data-binary @trace.jsonl`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The body is the trace. The response is the same text the command line prints: a summary
            line, a row per thread, and the ranked findings.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-file-html"></i> Self-contained HTML</h3>
          <pre><code>{`curl -sS -o report.html \\
  "${site.service}/analyze?format=html" \\
  --data-binary @trace.jsonl`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The HTML report is one file with nothing to fetch — the same artifact
            <span class="mono">--output report.html</span> produces locally.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-brackets-curly"></i> JSON, for a script</h3>
          <pre><code>{`curl -sS "${site.service}/analyze?format=json" \\
  --data-binary @trace.jsonl | jq .summary`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The same numbers as the report, as JSON, so a CI job can assert on them without parsing
            text.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-check-circle"></i> Verify only</h3>
          <pre><code>{`curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' \\
  "${site.service}/analyze?verify=1" \\
  --data-binary @trace.jsonl`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            200 when the decomposition holds on every sample, 422 when it does not. That is
            <span class="mono">pace verify</span> as a status code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <FormatFlow />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Endpoints and limits</div>
      <h2>Stated exactly, because guessing wastes your time</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-signpost"></i> Endpoints</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <tbody>
                {
                  endpoints.map(([method, path, what]) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="mono">{method}</span></td>
                      <td><span class="mono">{path}</span></td>
                      <td class="dim" style="white-space:normal">{what}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-gauge"></i> Limits</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <tbody>
                {
                  limits.map(([what, value]) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{what}</td>
                      <td class="dim" style="white-space:normal">{value}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">What happens to your trace</div>
        <p>
          Nothing is stored. The trace is read into memory for the duration of one request, the
          report is written to the response, and the buffer goes. There is no database behind this
          service and no persistent disk on the host — the free instance does not have one — so there
          is nowhere for a trace to end up even if something tried. If your trace is confidential,
          run the analyzer yourself; the
          {' '}<a href={href('install/')}>install page</a> takes one command.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">What it gives you back</div>
      <h2>The same decomposition, over the wire</h2>
      <GapDiagram />
      <DecompositionFigure />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Run it yourself</div>
      <h2>The service is not the product; the analyzer is</h2>
      <p>
        The hosted instance exists so you do not need a toolchain to see the output. Everything it
        does, a checkout does: the container definition and the service's own
        <span class="mono">render.yaml</span> are in the repository, so you can deploy the identical
        thing on your own host, or point the site at your own URL.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cube"></i> Container</h3>
          <pre><code>{`git clone ${site.repo}
cd PACE/service
docker build -t pace-service .
docker run -p 8080:8080 pace-service
curl localhost:8080/health`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-warning-circle"></i> Two honest caveats</h3>
          <p>
            The free instance has a tenth of a core and half a gigabyte of memory, so a very large
            trace will time out here and would not on your machine. And only one request is served at
            a time, so a queue behind a large trace is the expected behaviour rather than a bug.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither of those is a property of the method. If either matters to you, that is the point
            at which running it locally is the right answer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script is:inline define:vars={{ serviceUrl: site.service }}>
    // The service is on another origin, so this is a real CORS fetch. The
    // service sends Access-Control-Allow-Origin for GET /health, and the page
    // reports honestly when it cannot reach it.
    const set = (id, text) => {
      const el = document.getElementById(id);
      if (el) el.textContent = text;
    };
    (async () => {
      const started = performance.now();
      try {
        const response = await fetch(`${serviceUrl}/health`, { cache: 'no-store' });
        const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - started);
        if (!response.ok) {
          set('st-state', `answered ${response.status} in ${ms} ms`);
          return;
        }
        const body = await response.json();
        set('st-state', `awake — answered in ${ms} ms`);
        set('st-version', body.analyzer ?? '—');
        set('st-limit', body.sample_limit ? body.sample_limit.toLocaleString('en-US') : '—');
        const up = body.uptime_s;
        set(
          'st-uptime',
          typeof up === 'number'
            ? `${Math.floor(up / 60)} min ${Math.round(up % 60)} s (this instance, since it last started)`
            : '—'
        );
      } catch (error) {
        set('st-state', 'unreachable from your browser');
        set('st-version', '—');
      }
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
